<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/genetics-inheritance-patterns">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Mendel's insight — that traits pass as <strong>discrete particles, blended never</strong> —
    was 30 years ahead of its evidence and 90 ahead of its mechanism. This lesson is the working
    arithmetic: Punnett squares, dominant and recessive alleles, and the patterns pedigrees
    reveal. The DNA lesson told you what genes are made of; this one tells you how they play.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Punnett square in motion — four boxes, one ratio, a century of insight" widget="equationStep" config={punnettCfg} />

  <h2>Beyond the simple cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Incomplete dominance & codominance:</strong> red × white snapdragons give pink (blended expression); ABO blood types show both alleles at once (AB). "Dominant" means expressed, not stronger.</li>
    <li><strong>Sex linkage:</strong> genes on the X chromosome hit males harder — colour blindness and haemophilia run the classic mother-carrier paths.</li>
    <li><strong>Two genes at once:</strong> Mendel's 9:3:3:1 dihybrid ratio appears when genes sit on different chromosomes; the exceptions (linkage) became the first maps of chromosome geography.</li>
    <li><strong>Polygenic reality:</strong> height, skin tone, diabetes risk — thousands of genes plus environment, giving bell curves instead of ratios. Simple Mendelian cases are the exceptions that taught the rules.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendel, Versuche über Pflanzenhybriden (1866); OpenStax, Biology 2e (Patterns of Inheritance).</p>
</TopicPage>
